sv.h, sv.c: SV heads issued and released by an external lifetime adapter,
under PERL_SV_HEAD_ADAPTER. Without the define the preprocessed source is
unchanged.

Upstream carves SV heads from 4080-byte arenas (Perl_more_sv, sv_add_arena)
and keeps free ones on PL_sv_root, a list threaded through the freed head
itself (plant_SV writes SvANY). A stale SV* therefore still reaches a head
that the next new_SV hands to another value, and ASan cannot see it because
the memory never returns to malloc. To revoke or poison a released head, no
free-list link may live in it, and nothing may read it afterwards.

- Perl_more_sv takes each head from perl_svh_new(sizeof(SV), heads per arena).
  PL_sv_root is never set, so every new_SV reaches it, including the inline
  S_new_SV that -DDEBUGGING extensions (ext/re) compile. Upstream's geometry is
  passed along: an arena's first slot is its header, the rest are heads.
- plant_SV gives the head back with perl_svh_del; an SVf_BREAK head is not
  reusable, as upstream keeps it off the free list.
- A freed head's flags are SVTYPEMASK, and the core reads them through
  pointers that may be stale by design: SvIS_FREED (sv.h: "there are some
  parts of the core that have pointers to already-freed SV heads"), and type
  probes such as a COP's stash, PL_curstash, PL_last_in_gv restored by
  SAVESPTR, or GvIO's own test. Both SvIS_FREED and SvTYPE ask the adapter
  first, by address and current lease, and read the head only if it is live;
  a released head reads as SVTYPEMASK, which is what upstream reads there.
  SvTYPE's argument is still evaluated once.
- S_visit walks the adapter's live heads, newest arena first and ascending
  within one, over the arenas that existed on entry: upstream's order. A
  released head is known from the record and never read.
- sv_free_arenas has no head arenas to free. DEBUG_LEAKING_SCALARS, which walks
  them, is refused at compile time; S_del_sv's -DD arena check is skipped.

The adapter (capstone/ports/perl/sv-heads) keeps upstream's LIFO list and
arena growth, so a spatial build reissues the same slots in the same order.
A read of a released head that none of these paths covers faults under the
protected arms; the native ASan backend reports it (see the README).

